WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.020 --> 00:00:06.190
Of all the animals that we’ve examined in
the microcosmos, leeches are probably one

00:00:06.190 --> 00:00:09.921
of the few that can be used as a verb.

00:00:10.899 --> 00:00:15.975
I don’t know what it would mean to “tardigrade”
someone or something, but I do know what it

00:00:15.975 --> 00:00:18.129
means to leech off someone—

00:00:18.129 --> 00:00:23.340
to take and take from them, like a worm consuming
someone’s blood.

00:00:23.340 --> 00:00:28.219
It’s funny though, isn’t it, that we use
this word in such a derogatory way?

00:00:28.219 --> 00:00:33.610
We make an insult out of an animal we nearly
drove to extinction in some countries because

00:00:33.610 --> 00:00:43.026
we found them so useful, you might even ask
then, are we the leech, leeching off of leeches?

00:01:00.172 --> 00:01:04.349
Before we go too deep into sympathizing with
leeches, let’s be clear:

00:01:04.349 --> 00:01:07.830
leeches are very creepy for most of us anyway.

00:01:07.830 --> 00:01:13.570
James, our master of microscopes, has come
across leeches several times in his pond samples.

00:01:13.570 --> 00:01:18.830
A few years ago, he found two finger-length
black leeches in his samples and decided to

00:01:18.830 --> 00:01:23.208
put them in a container with the lid completely
sealed.

00:01:23.208 --> 00:01:28.220
His professor warned him that they would escape,
but James figured he'd closed the lid

00:01:28.220 --> 00:01:31.350
tight enough to ensure they would stay in
the container.

00:01:31.350 --> 00:01:35.849
So he left it in the bathroom and
he went to bed.

00:01:35.849 --> 00:01:40.490
The next morning, when he went to the bathroom,
he saw the one thing you don’t want to see

00:01:40.490 --> 00:01:43.790
when you have a container with leeches in
your home.

00:01:43.790 --> 00:01:50.009
The lid was on the floor, and the container
was empty.

00:01:50.009 --> 00:01:57.000
They had somehow managed to escape, and now
James was in a home with two kittens and a

00:01:57.000 --> 00:01:59.332
pair of wayward leeches.

00:02:03.278 --> 00:02:08.280
He searched through everything and to his
enormous relief, he finally managed to find

00:02:08.280 --> 00:02:12.993
the leeches behind his washing machine, covered
in dust bunnies.

00:02:12.993 --> 00:02:16.644
But at least he did learn 
to not underestimate them.

00:02:19.365 --> 00:02:24.558
These leeches we’re looking at now are from
a different genus than his runaway pair, but

00:02:24.558 --> 00:02:27.000
they also posed a challenge.

00:02:27.000 --> 00:02:33.019
They were able to attach themselves to the
glass of their fish tank with so much strength

00:02:33.019 --> 00:02:36.714
that James could not retrieve them like he
normally would.

00:02:36.714 --> 00:02:41.470
And when they did detach themselves from the
sides of the tank, the leeches would quickly

00:02:41.470 --> 00:02:45.251
swim down into the sediment and disappear.

00:02:45.251 --> 00:02:48.588
Eventually, James was able to get a hold of
them.

00:02:48.588 --> 00:02:51.380
But the struggles still continued.

00:02:51.380 --> 00:02:55.667
The leeches were simply not content to rest
on the slides.

00:02:55.667 --> 00:02:58.819
They would escape, they would crawl, they
would roll.

00:02:58.819 --> 00:03:01.962
One even jumped down from the microscope!

00:03:01.962 --> 00:03:07.641
Now James was finally able to rein them in through
a clever arrangement of slides and coverslips

00:03:07.641 --> 00:03:09.318
that let him record them.

00:03:09.318 --> 00:03:14.260
And we are both grateful for his persistence,
and very glad that none of us were in the

00:03:14.260 --> 00:03:17.252
room with him and those leeches.

00:03:17.252 --> 00:03:21.760
As we mentioned earlier, “leech” has the
distinction of referring both to an animal

00:03:21.760 --> 00:03:25.187
and an insult inspired by said animal.

00:03:25.187 --> 00:03:30.000
That might not be unusual when we look at
macroscopic animals, but animals of the microcosmos

00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:35.066
are of course so small that they don’t often
get the opportunity to permeate our language

00:03:35.066 --> 00:03:36.750
in many forms.

00:03:36.750 --> 00:03:42.684
The use of the word “leech” to describe a person 
as a parasite is only a few centuries old.

00:03:42.684 --> 00:03:49.519
But we have a long history with the word “leech,”
going back to at least the year 900 CE.

00:03:49.519 --> 00:03:53.417
In Old English, “leech” could mean one
of two things.

00:03:53.417 --> 00:03:57.340
One is the blood-sucking worm that we’re
looking at right now.

00:03:57.340 --> 00:04:00.071
The other meaning is “doctor”.

00:04:00.071 --> 00:04:04.945
The two definitions actually came into Old
English from two different origins, but they

00:04:04.945 --> 00:04:10.560
came to be intertwined in meaning the way
that leeches and doctors have been intertwined

00:04:10.560 --> 00:04:12.000
in practice.

00:04:12.000 --> 00:04:16.770
In the older understanding of medicine, built
upon the concept of humors that were transported

00:04:16.770 --> 00:04:22.144
around the body through blood, blood-letting
was seen as a way to restore balance.

00:04:22.144 --> 00:04:26.262
Excess blood, it was thought, could lead to
disease.

00:04:26.262 --> 00:04:30.480
And so removing that blood could be a medical
necessity.

00:04:30.480 --> 00:04:33.650
Of course, you could remove the blood through
surgical means.

00:04:33.650 --> 00:04:35.990
But leeches were a nice alternative.

00:04:35.990 --> 00:04:38.584
Slower, not quite as painful.

00:04:38.584 --> 00:04:44.189
And there are some afflictions where a patient
might prefer a worm to a doctor stabbing them.

00:04:44.189 --> 00:04:49.040
The main downside was the need to prevent
leeches from crawling into the patient through

00:04:49.040 --> 00:04:51.970
their mouth, nose, or anus.

00:04:53.112 --> 00:04:57.979
There are actually hundreds of leech species, 
but the one used for medical purposes was

00:04:57.979 --> 00:05:03.348
named Hirudo medicinalis 
in 1758 by Carl Linnaeus.

00:05:03.348 --> 00:05:09.210
In the wild, this worm feed on mammals,
fish, and amphibians, tracking movements in

00:05:09.210 --> 00:05:13.460
the water along with heat and chemical signals
to find their host.

00:05:13.460 --> 00:05:20.350
Then, it uses its three jaws and the
sharp teeth lining them to pierce the skin.

00:05:20.350 --> 00:05:26.750
The leech will usually consume anywhere from 2 to
5 times their body weight in blood, relying

00:05:26.750 --> 00:05:32.050
on a cocktail of enzymes and other molecules
in their saliva to keep the blood flowing

00:05:32.050 --> 00:05:33.599
and not clotting.

00:05:33.599 --> 00:05:38.392
When they’re done, the leech might take
months to digest that meal.

00:05:38.392 --> 00:05:44.765
Around the 18th and 19th centuries, using
leeches as a medical tool ballooned in popularity,

00:05:44.765 --> 00:05:49.800
thanks in part to the influence of the
French physician Francois-Joseph-Victor Broussais

00:05:49.800 --> 00:05:53.534
which led to a massive trade in the worm across
Europe.

00:05:53.534 --> 00:05:57.920
If you read about the history of leeching,
you’ll find all sorts of terrifying statistics

00:05:57.920 --> 00:05:59.520
describing their use.

00:05:59.520 --> 00:06:07.358
For example, in 1832, France was estimated
to have imported more than 50 million leeches.

00:06:09.984 --> 00:06:15.400
Imagine transporting hundreds 
of thousands of leeches.

00:06:15.400 --> 00:06:17.410
Or maybe you shouldn’t imagine that.

00:06:17.410 --> 00:06:22.750
But of course, trading leeches also meant
catching them, which is easy when you’re

00:06:22.750 --> 00:06:25.610
the exact host that leeches are looking for.

00:06:25.610 --> 00:06:31.520
Leech hunters would simply use their feet
as bait, extracting the worms from their own

00:06:31.520 --> 00:06:34.529
bodies to be able to send them on their way.

00:06:34.529 --> 00:06:37.443
And look, leeches weren’t some rare animal.

00:06:37.443 --> 00:06:42.759
But when people began trading them in such
massive quantities, that changed.

00:06:42.759 --> 00:06:47.349
That was a problem for trade, but also for
wild leech populations.

00:06:47.349 --> 00:06:52.740
Some countries approached this problem by trying
to build leech farms that could reduce the

00:06:52.740 --> 00:06:54.652
need for wild leeches.

00:06:54.652 --> 00:07:00.766
Others tried to raise prices or limit exports,
sometimes even banning them.

00:07:00.766 --> 00:07:03.757
But something else was changing as well.

00:07:03.757 --> 00:07:09.180
Scientists started doubting whether leeches
were really that great of a medical tool to

00:07:09.180 --> 00:07:10.339
begin with.

00:07:10.339 --> 00:07:15.879
The most notable opponent was the French physician
Pierre Charles Alexandre Louis, who argued

00:07:15.879 --> 00:07:20.732
that leeching might actually be leaving patients
in worse conditions.

00:07:20.732 --> 00:07:24.930
And this was the century where scientists
began to consider an alternate explanation

00:07:24.930 --> 00:07:32.253
for disease, one built not on balances of
humors, but rather on the existence of germs.

00:07:32.253 --> 00:07:38.170
And that opened up new approaches to treatment,
one that did not rely on latching a blood-sucking

00:07:38.170 --> 00:07:40.748
worm onto people’s skin.

00:07:40.748 --> 00:07:46.190
So the leech trade eventually died out, but
the combination of our use of leeches and

00:07:46.190 --> 00:07:52.568
the decline of their wetland habitat have left
its mark on the Hirudo medicinalis population.

00:07:52.568 --> 00:07:58.310
At first, it seemed like they may have gone
extinct entirely in Western Europe, but reports

00:07:58.310 --> 00:08:01.707
of their demise seem to have been exaggerated.

00:08:01.707 --> 00:08:04.269
Not that the reality is that much better.

00:08:04.269 --> 00:08:08.420
If you look up their status in various countries
in Western Europe, you’ll see every label

00:08:08.420 --> 00:08:13.634
from not threatened to rare to extinct to
unknown.

00:08:13.634 --> 00:08:16.814
But they have made a comeback in medicine.

00:08:16.814 --> 00:08:21.150
Though not to the same degree that they were used
before, but Hirudo medicinalis and their close

00:08:21.150 --> 00:08:26.750
relative Hirudo verbena are used in hospitals
for certain treatments, like draining the

00:08:26.750 --> 00:08:29.014
clotted blood of haematomas.

00:08:29.014 --> 00:08:34.000
And aside from their medical uses, leeches
have a well-studied system of ganglia—

00:08:34.000 --> 00:08:37.310
the cluster of neurons that you can see here.

00:08:37.310 --> 00:08:42.830
And that has made leeches a useful model for
scientists to use to better understand the

00:08:42.830 --> 00:08:47.533
interplay between an animal’s nervous system
and its behavior.

00:08:47.533 --> 00:08:52.121
So our history continues to be entwined with
the leech.

00:08:52.121 --> 00:08:56.880
And while we started by asking whether we are
really the leech here, maybe that’s

00:08:56.880 --> 00:08:58.436
too simple a question.

00:08:58.436 --> 00:09:04.870
This is an animal whose life is intrinsically
connected to ours through blood, a relationship

00:09:04.870 --> 00:09:09.264
forged through evolution and reshaped by history.

00:09:09.264 --> 00:09:14.099
So maybe the question isn’t one we can answer,
but rather something open-ended:

00:09:14.099 --> 00:09:18.960
if a leech has been a worm, a parasite, a
doctor, an action—

00:09:18.960 --> 00:09:22.890
what else can it be that we don’t yet know?

00:09:27.743 --> 00:09:34.098
Thank you for coming on this journey with us as 
we explore the unseen world that surrounds us.

00:09:34.098 --> 00:09:38.040
The people whose names are on the screen right
now, they are our Patreon patrons.

00:09:38.040 --> 00:09:41.433
The people who make it possible for us to
make this content.

00:09:41.433 --> 00:09:46.050
And while some of them may be questioning
their decision, considering how much leech

00:09:46.050 --> 00:09:51.863
content just got thrown at them, I think that
most of them are very satisfied with the decision.

00:09:51.863 --> 00:09:56.269
We certainly keep our patrons for a long time
and we very much appreciate that.

00:09:56.269 --> 00:10:03.243
If you would like to become one of our Patreon
patrons, you can join us at Patreon.com/JourneytoMicro.

00:10:03.243 --> 00:10:07.240
If you want to see more from our Master of
